  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 952 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nVALLEY RAIN AND MOUNTAIN SNOW THROUGH WEDNESDAY NIGHT:\n\nA low pressure system will bring widespread precipitation to \neastern Utah and western Colorado through late Wednesday night, \nincluding valley rain and mountain snow. Water vapor imagery \nshows this low is currently centered along the Utah/Wyoming \nborder, and is allowing for a steady stream of moisture-rich \nsouthwesterly flow from the Four Corners up through northwest \nColorado.\n\nAs we move into the overnight hours, precipitation over the \nhigher terrain is expected to transition to snow or a rain/snow \nmix as temperatures cool. The center of the low pressure system \nwill move across western Colorado during the day Wednesday, \nproviding substantial lift as it does so. Peak snowfall rates in\nthe southern mountains are forecast to occur between 6 AM and \n12 PM Wednesday, with light snowfall through Wednesday night. In\nthe central and northern Colorado mountains, snowfall rates \nwill increase Wednesday morning, with peak rates are expected \nbetween noon and midnight as the low lifts northward. Snow \nlevels look to drop to 8,000-10,000 ft Wednesday morning, but \nrelatively warm road conditions leading up to this event \nintroduces uncertainty about how much snow will accumulate, \nespecially as temperatures warm and snow levels rise during the \nday. Melting and wet snow could result in slushy road conditions\nand reduced visibilities over mountain passes, so be sure to \nuse caution if traveling. Elevation-based Winter Weather \nAdvisories have been issued for the San Juan Mountains and for \nColorado's northern and central mountains beginning 6 AM \nWednesday. \n\nCOOL AND DRY FOR THE LATTER HALF OF THE WEEK:\n\nDrier air moves into the region after the low pressure system \nmoves east of the Rocky Mountains on Thursday morning. The \ncurrent forecast keeps precipitation chances low Thursday \nafternoon through Sunday, especially outside of the higher \nterrain areas. A much cooler airmass is left in its wake, and \ncould lead to the first hard freeze of the year across northwest\nColorado on Thursday night. Daytime highs will run around 5-10 \ndegrees below normal through the end of the work week, with more\nseasonable temperatures slated to return this weekend.",
